<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Galerius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 258–311</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Galerius was a Roman emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetrarchy">Tetrarchy</a>, made Caesar under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> in 293 and senior emperor in the east from 305 to 311. He fought on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> against the Carpi and other peoples and won a major victory over the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narseh">Narseh</a> in 298. A leading force behind the Great Persecution of Christians, he ended it by an edict of toleration shortly before his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galerius.jpg" alt="Galerius" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shinjirod · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Romuliana_Galerius_head.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jews were at times exempt from official sacrifice, but Christians were persecuted: by Nero after the fire of 64 CE, under Decius’s edict of sacrifice, by Valerian, and by Diocletian’s edicts from 303, until <b>Galerius</b> revoked the policy in 311.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Several Roman and Byzantine emperors were wholly or partly of Thracian descent, among them Maximinus Thrax, <b>Galerius</b> and Leo I the Thracian, and Constantine the Great was born to a Thracian father of the Moesi tribe.</blockquote><a href="/culture/thracians">Thracians · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Gravely ill, Emperor <b>Galerius</b> granted Christians the right to worship, ending the Great Persecution in his territories.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-30">30 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Galerius</b> granted him only the title caesar, which Constantine accepted to remove doubts about his legitimacy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-25">25 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At Milan Maximian gave Constantius the office of Caesar, and the same day Diocletian did the same for <b>Galerius</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-1">1 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In ceremonies at Nicomedia and Milan, the two senior emperors stepped down in favour of <b>Galerius</b> and Constantius.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-1">1 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
